## F-011 continued: does the trigger file's own prose still claim what it now is?
$ python3: print schema_note / split_policy / negative_design_note / any new key at b2827ce
  [skill_name]
    devforge-design

  [revision]
    2

  [revision_note]
    Revision 2, repair pass 1 (CHG-011, finding F-011). Query A7b - "Change the accepted requirement that says out-of-area postcodes are rejected at signup." - moved from negative_define_product to a new negative_change category, matching the description's routing of an accepted-requirement change to devforge-change. Its should_trigger and split values are unchanged, as is every other entry. Re-cutting the stratification invalidates no measurement: no tier-A run has been performed against revision 1. Known limit: negative_change now holds one entry, on the validation side, so it contributes no train-side signal - an owner allocating tier A may want a second query in it first. Revision 1 is the authored file at commit 9ad38de.

  [schema_note]
    Each entry follows docs/mvp/templates/skill-authoring/trigger-queries.json (id, query, should_trigger, split) plus a category field. Tier A only: these observe whether the terminal consults the skill for a request that does not name it. No entry supplies the skill path or name except the explicit_invocation category, which is recorded separately and is never counted as implicit activation evidence.

  [split_policy]
    Fixed and stratified by should_trigger and category, assigned once at authoring time and recorded here. It is not re-randomized per run. Validation entries and their should_trigger values must not be placed in an author-loop or task-worker context; only the train split may drive any description iteration.

  [negative_design_note]
    The word 'design' is heavily overloaded in software work. The four negative_non_ui_design queries exist specifically to separate the user experience from schema design, API design, pattern selection and system design; the other four negative categories - negative_prototype, negative_develop, negative_define_product and negative_change - separate this skill from the sibling that owns each of those requests, and negative_ordinary_task covers everyday work that is not a DevForgeAI phase at all. A near-miss that fails here is a description defect, not a model defect.

  A7a / A7b / A4* entries:
    A4a: cat=negative_non_ui_design split=train trig=False :: Design the database schema for the loans table.
    A4b: cat=negative_non_ui_design split=train trig=False :: Help me design the REST API for borrow requests - what should the endpoints and payloads be?
